# Cloudflare Pages redirects
#
# Intentionally empty of an /admin rule.
#
# Netlify needed `/admin  /admin.html  200` to serve the admin page at a clean
# URL. On Pages that rule causes an INFINITE REDIRECT LOOP: Pages already serves
# /admin from admin.html by itself, and its built-in HTML handling rewrites the
# rule's own target /admin.html back to /admin — so the rule bounces the request
# between the two forever (verified: 50 redirects, never resolves).
#
# If you add rules later, avoid targeting a *.html path that Pages will itself
# redirect to its extension-less form.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Keep the workshop out of the shop window.
#
# Cloudflare Pages publishes the WHOLE repository. There is no ignore file that
# changes that: a leading dot does not hide a directory (verified — /.tests/ is
# served), and .assetsignore is a Workers Static Assets feature that Pages does
# not read (also verified, then deleted rather than left lying).
#
# The only two real options are a build output directory or these rules. These
# rules were chosen because they need no build step, and a build step is a new
# way for a deploy to fail on a site that is about to be handed over.
#
# This works because of one documented guarantee: "Redirects are always
# followed, regardless of whether or not an asset matches the incoming request."
# So the file can still sit in the repo and still not be readable over the web.
# 404 is not a status _redirects supports, so these are 301s to the homepage.
#
# Adding a new internal document? Add it here in the same commit.

/handover.html    /  301
/README.md        /  301
/CLOUDFLARE.md    /  301
/PRICING.md       /  301
/SETUP.md         /  301
/MAIL-DNS.md      /  301
/package.json     /  301
/.gitignore       /  301

/brainstorms/*    /  301
/.tests/*         /  301
/.wiseai/*        /  301
/.claude/*        /  301
/.netlify/*       /  301
